config STATUS
	bool "status: status codes and contract assertions"
	default y
	help
	  The vocabulary every other module shares: the el_status codes
	  returned for recoverable failures, and the EL_ASSERT() check used
	  for contract violations.

if STATUS

config ASSERT
	bool "Check contract assertions"
	default y
	help
	  Compile EL_ASSERT() checks into Elemental's modules. They catch
	  programmer mistakes at the interface: null pointers, misuse of an
	  object, and backends that break their contract.

	  With this off, the checks compile to nothing and violating a
	  documented precondition is undefined behavior. Turn it off only for
	  an image that has been validated with it on and cannot afford the
	  code size; the cost on a Cortex-M3 build is recorded in
	  docs/build.md.

choice ASSERT_HANDLER
	prompt "Assertion failure handler"
	default ASSERT_HANDLER_TRAP
	depends on ASSERT
	help
	  What a failed EL_ASSERT() does.

config ASSERT_HANDLER_TRAP
	bool "Trap at the failing check"
	help
	  Execute a trap instruction where the check failed. This needs no
	  runtime support and stores no strings: a debugger or fault handler
	  sees the exact location. The right default for firmware and for
	  hosted development alike.

config ASSERT_HANDLER_APPLICATION
	bool "Call el_assert_failed(), defined by the application"
	help
	  Call el_assert_failed(file, line), which the application must
	  define and which must not return. Use this to route failures into
	  a product's fault handling, or to report where a check failed.
	  Linking fails if the function is missing.

	  Each check then carries a file name and line number, which costs
	  read-only data.

endchoice

endif # STATUS
